description: Guaranteed Hard Exit — logout and recovery must work without API or router
alwaysApply: true
---

# Hard Exit Architecture Standard

This application must always provide a guaranteed escape route from a broken application state.

## Core Principle

Hard Exit must not depend on:

* API availability
* Auth providers
* Router state
* Realtime state
* WebSocket state
* Background jobs

Exit must always work.

## Required Behavior

When implementing logout, emergency exit, room exit, session recovery, or fatal error recovery:

Prefer Hard Exit over standard logout.

## Standard Flow

User Click Exit
↓
Set Hard Exit Flag
↓
Block UI
↓
Stop Polling
↓
Stop Realtime
↓
Stop WebSockets
↓
Stop Background Jobs
↓
Clear Stores
↓
Clear Cache
↓
Clear Session
↓
Fire SignOut Async
↓
Force Redirect

## Idempotency Rule

Hard Exit must be idempotent.

Multiple calls must produce a single execution.

Use:

isHardExiting()

or equivalent protection.

## Cleanup Requirements

Stop:

* Supabase Realtime
* WebSockets
* SSE
* Firebase listeners
* SignalR connections

Stop:

* setInterval
* setTimeout loops
* React Query polling
* SWR refresh
* custom pollers

Clear:

* Zustand
* Redux
* Context state
* Local stores

Clear:

* Auth tokens
* Session cookies
* LocalStorage
* SessionStorage
* Cached user data

## Navigation Rule

Never rely on:

router.push('/login')

For Hard Exit.

Prefer:

window.location.replace('/login')

or

window.location.href = '/login'

## SignOut Rule

Do not wait for signOut completion.

Run asynchronously.

Example:

supabase.auth.signOut().catch(() => {})

## Timeout Fallback

Always keep a redirect fallback timer.

Example:

setTimeout(() => {
window.location.replace('/login')
}, 750)

## Global Event

Publish:

app:hard-exit

Allow listeners to perform cleanup.

## Observability

Log:

[HardExit] Started
[HardExit] Realtime Stopped
[HardExit] State Cleared
[HardExit] Redirecting

## Use Cases

Apply this pattern for:

* Logout
* Emergency Exit
* Session Recovery
* Broken Realtime
* Room Corruption
* Infinite Loading
* Fatal Client Errors
* PWA Recovery

## Final Principle

No matter what fails:

Exit must always work.
